# Ode to Joy (theme), Ludwig van Beethoven, Symphony no. 9 in D minor, op. 125, 4th movement.
# The melody is public domain (Beethoven died in 1827). Theme as first stated by the cellos and basses
# (bar 92 onwards; public-domain edition: Breitkopf & Haertel, Beethovens Werke, Serie 1 no. 9, 1863,
# on IMSLP). Transposed from D major to G major with the tonic at G5 (Story 1.6, owner decision 2).
# Note sequence checked against the G major melody excerpt of the English Wikipedia article
# "Ode to Joy" (read 2026-11-21). Rhythm: the SIMPLIFIED COMMON ARRANGEMENT (owner decision 6,
# 2026-11-21): bar 12 is G A D5(half) and the last phrase starts with two B attacks on beats 1 and 2
# of bar 13. Beethoven's original has a syncopated entry instead (G A D5 quarters, then the B5 on
# beat 4 of bar 12 tied over the bar line); same notes, same length. Only the notes are taken.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT (this transcription file; the melody itself is public domain).
#
# Format (Story 1.6, read by spikes/gen_trials_1_6.py): one note per line, `<pitch> <beats>`.
#   <pitch> is the SOUNDING pitch (profile convention: A5 = MIDI 81), name + optional # or b + octave.
#   <beats> is a decimal number of quarter-note beats (1 = quarter, 0.5 = eighth, 1.5 = dotted quarter).
#   `R <beats>` is a rest. `#` starts a comment. A tied note is written as one note with the summed beats.
# 4/4, 16 bars, 64 beats, 62 notes. Range D5 (74) to D6 (86).

